MAJOR_VERSION=5
STANDARD_VERSION=1
RELEASE_VERSION=0
DESCRIPTION<<PORTFOLIO_NOTIFY_DESC_END
5.1.0: Flintstone now tells the portfolio when it has published, instead of the portfolio having to be told by hand.

Adds .github/workflows/notify-portfolio.yml, which sends a repository_dispatch event of type project-updated to BPForbes/Bailey-Forbes-Portfolio-Site.github.io so bailey-forbes.com re-queries GitHub, regenerates the Flintstone metadata it displays (version, commit and pull request counts, Linguist language balance, and release milestones read from version/locked/*.ver), and redeploys. No Flintstone state is sent in the payload: the event carries only the repository, commit, ref, workflow name, and project id, and the portfolio remains responsible for deriving every number it prints. Nothing about the kernel, the build, or the promotion gates changes.

Timing is fail-closed against the existing promotion path. The workflow waits on Browser kernel artifact completing on main, but that workflow concluding successfully is not by itself evidence that anything was published: the deploy job is conditional on bootable, browser_compatible, and boot_smoke_passed, so a candidate that builds and tests cleanly while failing a promotion gate records a blocked outcome, leaves validate green, and skips deploy, leaving the run green with the lab unchanged. A gate job therefore reads back what the job named "Publish validated lab to GitHub Pages" actually concluded for that run, through repos/{repo}/actions/runs/{id}/jobs, and the notification is sent only on success. A skipped or failed publish stops it, so the portfolio can never advertise a lab revision that visitors cannot open.

The other workflows are deliberately not wired: CI runs on develop and feature branches and validates versioning rather than publishing, Version lock on merge pushes to develop and produces no artifact, and Deploy is workflow_dispatch only with no Pages state.

Least privilege throughout. The gate job takes actions: read and nothing else; the notify job calls a reusable workflow in the portfolio repository that declares permissions {} because the dispatch is an outbound call. Authentication is a new repository secret, PORTFOLIO_DISPATCH_TOKEN, holding a fine-grained token scoped to the single portfolio repository with Contents write, which is the narrowest permission the dispatch endpoint accepts. It is read from the environment rather than interpolated into a command line, is never echoed, and reaches no other repository. The dispatch implementation is shared rather than copied per project, so the Flintstone-side configuration is the trigger, the gate, and the call.
PORTFOLIO_NOTIFY_DESC_END
